(* Quest Self-Hosted Compiler: The Register Allocator's Interface
 *
 * Gives each virtual register of a machine function (codegen/machine) one of
 * the target's physical registers (codegen/target), or memory, by linear scan
 * with interval splitting (after Wimmer and Mossenbock, "Optimized Interval
 * Splitting in a Linear Scan Register Allocator"; docs/arm64.md section 3.8).
 * The target is a parameter, so that a test (regalloc_scan) can run the
 * allocator on a toy target with a few registers.
 *
 * Linear scan, compared with coloring: A coloring allocator builds an
 * interference graph, with an edge between two values live at the same time,
 * and colors it with the registers. Linear scan lays the code out in one
 * line, numbers its positions, and describes each value by its lifetime
 * interval: the positions where it is live. Two values interfere exactly when
 * their intervals intersect. Taking the intervals in order of their starts,
 * it gives each one a register that no interval already in that register
 * intersects. It never builds the graph and never backtracks, so it is fast;
 * its choices are greedy, and can be worse than coloring's.
 *
 * Positions: The blocks are laid out in reverse postorder, so a block comes
 * after the blocks that jump to it, except a loop's header, which a later
 * block jumps back to. Each block's start and each instruction get two
 * positions. An instruction at position p reads its operands at p and writes
 * its results at p + 1, so a result does not intersect an operand read for
 * the last time at p, and may take its register.
 *
 * Intervals: An interval is a list of ranges of positions, with holes between
 * them where the value is not live (say, in the other arm of an if); another
 * interval can use its register in a hole. The ranges come from liveness over
 * the blocks. The positions where the value is read (its uses) are recorded
 * too, for the choices below.
 *
 * The scan: In order of their starts, each interval gets:
 *
 *   - a register free for all of it, if there is one: first one that a hint
 *     (below) names, then a caller-saved one;
 *   - otherwise, if a register is free where it starts, the one free the
 *     longest; the interval is split where that register stops being free,
 *     and the rest is spilled (below);
 *   - otherwise, a register taken from the intervals holding it: the register
 *     whose holders cost least to spill (below), which are split where they
 *     meet the interval and spilled from there; unless the interval itself
 *     costs less, when it is the one spilled (at equal costs, the one next
 *     read furthest away is).
 *
 * The second case also takes a register if its holders cost less to spill
 * than the part of the interval it would spill.
 *
 * A spilled interval, or part of one, is in memory only until its next read;
 * there the rest is split off and allocated again, as if it were a new
 * interval.
 *
 * Costs: Spilling an interval from a position costs the load at its next
 * read, so the frequency of that read's block, which is estimated: 1000,
 * times 8 for each loop it is in (up to four), or 1 for a block the target
 * says is cold (one from which every path raises). Where frequencies are
 * equal, as in code without loops, the choices are "furthest next read"; a
 * value read next in a loop keeps a register over one read next outside it,
 * however near.

 * Unavailable registers: A register is not free for an interval where:
 *
 *   - a physical operand fixes it: the calling convention's moves to argument
 *     registers before a call, and from result registers after it, fix each
 *     register from the instruction that writes it to the last that reads it;
 *   - a call clobbers it: a value live across a call cannot be in a
 *     caller-saved register after the call;
 *   - a raise enters: every register is dead at the start of a block that a
 *     raise enters (where a try lands, before jumping to its handler), so a
 *     value live there is in memory there.
 *
 * Hints: A move from one register to another, and a jump's argument and the
 * parameter of its target that it is passed to, are hints that the two should
 * share a register. An interval takes the register of what it is hinted to,
 * if that one is free for all of it, and the move then costs nothing. This is
 * what coalescing does in a coloring allocator.
 *
 * Pieces: Thus a value lives in pieces, each in one register or in memory.
 * Since the code is in SSA form, every virtual register is defined once: a
 * value with a piece in memory is stored to its stack slot once, where it is
 * defined, and the slot holds it wherever it is live. Going from a register
 * to memory costs nothing. Going back costs a load at the start of the piece,
 * which is just before an instruction that reads the value; or, for a value
 * that an instruction computes from no virtual register, such as a constant,
 * that instruction again (rematerialized), and the value has no slot at all.
 * A piece that starts at a block's start is reached on each edge into the
 * block, whose code moves each value live there from where it is at the
 * edge's source: before a jump, or at the start of a branch's target, which
 * has no other predecessor.
 *
 * rewrite then makes the instructions use the registers chosen: an operand in
 * memory is loaded into one of the target's scratch registers first, a result
 * in memory is written to one and stored, and the moves between pieces and on
 * edges are made, in an order that has the effect of making them all at once
 * (a parallel move), through the target's Lowering. *)

interface Regalloc
import
    targets = questlang/codegen/target : questlang/codegen/Target
    questlang/codegen/machine : questlang/codegen/Machine
export
    (* Where a value is when it is in memory rather than a register *)
    memory: Int

    (* A move just before the instruction at position at, where a virtual
     * register's (value, by its index) piece in register to starts, and its
     * previous piece was in from: another register, or memory *)
    Def Move = Tuple at: Int value: Int from: Int to: Int end

    Def Allocation = Tuple
        (* Each virtual register's pieces, by its index: where each starts, in
         * order, and its register, or memory *)
        starts: Array(Array(Int))
        homes: Array(Array(Int))
        (* Whether one has a piece in memory, and so is stored there when it
         * is defined, unless it is rematerialized (one with no pieces, never
         * live, is in memory everywhere but never stored, as is one never
         * read) *)
        spilled: Array(Bool)
        (* Whether one is rematerialized rather than stored *)
        remat: Array(Bool)
        (* The moves to pieces in registers that start inside blocks, in order
         * of their positions, each made just before the instruction there *)
        moves: Array(Move)
        (* Each block's first position, negative for one not reached *)
        blockStart: Array(Int)
        (* What each block's edges in must move: the virtual registers (by
         * index) live into it *)
        liveIn: Array(Array(Int))
        (* The callee-saved registers given to any, which the function must
         * save and restore for its caller *)
        calleeUsed: Array(Int)
        (* Each block's estimated frequency *)
        frequency: Array(Int)
        (* Each virtual register's first and last positions, by its index,
         * over all its pieces: where its slot, if it has one, holds it *)
        first: Array(Int)
        last: Array(Int)
    end

    (* The allocation of a function's virtual registers to the target's
     * allocatable registers (a call preserves the callee-saved ones and no
     * others). rematerializable says which instructions can be made again
     * wherever their result is needed, rather than the result stored; only
     * those that read no virtual register and write one are asked. cold says
     * which blocks are cold. *)
    allocate(f: machine.Function t: targets.Target rematerializable: All(i: machine.Inst) Bool cold: All(b: Int) Bool):
        Allocation
    (* Where a virtual register (by its index) is at a position: its register,
     * or memory *)
    home(a: Allocation v: Int p: Int): Int
    (* Whether a virtual register (by index) is stored in its memory when it
     * is defined, and so needs a stack slot *)
    needsSlot(a: Allocation v: Int): Bool

    (* The slot each virtual register that needs one is stored in (by its
     * index; ~1 for one that needs none), numbered from 0: registers of the
     * same kind (by register) share a slot where their lifetimes do not
     * overlap, counting the places rewrite writes a slot outside its value's
     * lifetime (a block parameter's, on the jumps into its block, and a clear
     * after its value dies), so that a frame holds the most values in memory
     * at once rather than every value ever there. Not for a function a raise
     * enters, whose handler reads slots where liveness does not see it. *)
    shareSlots(f: machine.Function a: Allocation lowering: machine.Lowering kind: All(v: Int) Int): Array(Int)

    (* The function, its virtual registers replaced by those allocated, the
     * loads, stores, and moves added *)
    rewrite(f: machine.Function a: Allocation lowering: machine.Lowering): Ok
end
